From: Escapement
Subject: [PATCH] dts: name the side buttons, and make the PMU interrupt usable

## Naming

LilyGo label these "BOOT" and "PWR". Both are wrong for a user of the watch:
"BOOT" describes what GPIO0 does during reset, not what the button is, and the
bottom button is the general-purpose one people will actually reach for.

Named by position instead, with the horological word for a watch button that
is not the crown:

    top-left     Pusher A   AXP2101 power key   (was "PWR")
    bottom-left  Pusher B   SoC GPIO0, active low (was "BOOT")
    near USB-C   Reset      CHIP_EN, hardware   (not visible to software)

Established by measurement, not by reading the silkscreen: polling GPIO0 while
pressing each button in turn showed the BOTTOM-LEFT one changing it, and the
top-left one not.

## The PMU interrupt needed a pull-up

`interrupts = <7 IRQ_TYPE_LEVEL_LOW>` alone hung the boot: the line floated,
read as asserted, and re-fired forever. Adding a pinctrl entry with input-enable
and internal pull-up fixes it, and the boot now completes with the interrupt
live.

## Pusher B is polled

`gpiod_to_irq()` returns -ENXIO for GPIO 0 on this SoC. It is not a general
failure: the RTC on GPIO1 and the PMU on GPIO7 both get real virtual IRQs. Pin 0
specifically does not, so Pusher B uses gpio-keys-polled at 50 ms -- which is
what the ESP-IDF firmware does anyway.

`KEY_ENTER` because there is no better fit in the key code space for a
general-purpose button, and userspace binds meaning, not the kernel.

## Verified on hardware

Both devices produce real events, captured from /dev/input/eventN:

    event0  Pusher B   01 00 1c 00 01 00   EV_KEY code 28 (KEY_ENTER) press
                       01 00 1c 00 00 00   release

    event1  Pusher A   01 00 74 00 01 00   EV_KEY code 116 (KEY_POWER) press
                       01 00 74 00 00 00   release

Pusher A delivering KEY_POWER is also the proof that the PMU interrupt works
end to end: axp20x-pek only reports on the PEK edge interrupts, so those edges
are reaching the kernel through esp32xx-gpio.

--- a/arch/xtensa/boot/dts/esp32s3-devkit-c1.dts	2026-11-08 13:31:58.614772362 -0700
+++ arch/xtensa/boot/dts/esp32s3-devkit-c1.dts	2026-11-08 13:36:50.728499173 -0700
@@ -2,6 +2,7 @@
 /dts-v1/;
 #include <dt-bindings/gpio/gpio.h>
 #include <dt-bindings/interrupt-controller/irq.h>
+#include <dt-bindings/input/linux-event-codes.h>
 #include "gpio-esp32.h"
 #include "esp32s3.dtsi"
 
@@ -56,6 +57,12 @@
 		pmic@34 {
 			compatible = "x-powers,axp2101";
 			reg = <0x34>;
+			pinctrl-0 = <&pmic_irq_pins>;
+			pinctrl-names = "default";
+			interrupt-parent = <&gpio0>;
+			interrupts = <7 IRQ_TYPE_LEVEL_LOW>;
+			interrupt-controller;
+			#interrupt-cells = <1>;
 
 			battery {
 				compatible = "x-powers,axp2101-battery-power-supply";
@@ -80,6 +87,36 @@
 	 * gpio 7 on the expander is P07 (linear numbering: P00..P07 = 0..7,
 	 * P10..P17 = 8..15).
 	 */
+	/*
+	 * The side buttons, named by position rather than by what LilyGo call
+	 * them. "BOOT" describes what GPIO0 does during reset, not what the
+	 * button is; the user presses it while wearing the watch.
+	 *
+	 * Pusher A is the top-left button. It is not a GPIO: it is the AXP2101
+	 * power key, so it arrives through axp20x-pek (see the pmic node).
+	 * Pusher B is the bottom-left button, GPIO0, active low.
+	 * The recessed button by the USB port is the SoC's chip-enable and is
+	 * not visible to software at all.
+	 */
+	gpio-keys {
+		/* polled, not interrupt: the esp32 GPIO driver returns -ENXIO
+		 * from gpiod_to_irq() for GPIO 0 (interrupts on pins 1 and 7
+		 * work, so the irq domain itself is fine). Polling at 50 ms is
+		 * what the ESP-IDF firmware does anyway.
+		 */
+		compatible = "gpio-keys-polled";
+		pinctrl-0 = <&pusher_b_pins>;
+		pinctrl-names = "default";
+		poll-interval = <50>;
+		autorepeat;
+
+		pusher-b {
+			label = "Pusher B";
+			gpios = <&gpio0 0 GPIO_ACTIVE_LOW>;
+			linux,code = <KEY_ENTER>;
+		};
+	};
+
 	display_vcc: regulator-display {
 		compatible = "regulator-fixed";
 		regulator-name = "display";
@@ -100,6 +137,14 @@
 			PIN(3) FUN0_20MA_IE_WPU			/* SDA */
 			PIN(2) FUN0_20MA_IE_WPU>;		/* SCL */
 	};
+	pusher_b_pins: pusher_b_pins {
+		pinctrl-single,pins = <
+			PIN(0) FUN0_20MA_IE_WPU>;		/* Pusher B, active low */
+	};
+	pmic_irq_pins: pmic_irq_pins {
+		pinctrl-single,pins = <
+			PIN(7) FUN0_20MA_IE_WPU>;		/* AXP2101 IRQ, open drain */
+	};
 	spi2_pins: spi2_pins {
 		pinctrl-single,pins = <
 			PIN(10) (FUN_SEL(4) | FUN_DRV_20MA)	/* CS0 */
